{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc6zMDqy9hTt1CbGFrXm", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "`git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> [<branch>]` replays the commits that are in `<branch>` but not in `<upstream>` on top of `<newbase>`. In other words, it lets you move a range of commits to a different base, which is something a plain `git rebase` can't do. A plain rebase always uses the upstream as the new base.\n\n**Example**\n\nSay you branched `feature` off `topic`, and `topic` itself branched off `main`:\n\n```\nmain:    A---B---C\n                  \\\ntopic:             D---E\n                        \\\nfeature:                 F---G\n```\n\nYou want `feature` to contain only F and G, and to sit directly on `main`, without D and E:\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto main topic feature\n```\n\n- `main` is the new base.\n- `topic` is the old base. Commits reachable from `feature` but not from `topic` (F and G) are the ones that get replayed.\n- `feature` is the branch to rebase. If you leave it out, the current branch is used.\n\nResult:\n\n```\nmain:    A---B---C\n                 |\\\n                 | D---E          (topic, unchanged)\n                  \\\nfeature:           F'---G'\n```\n\nF' and G' are new commits with the same changes as F and G but different hashes.\n\n**Common uses**\n- Moving a branch off another in-progress branch, as above.\n- Dropping a range of commits: `git rebase --onto A B` removes the commits after `A` up to `B` from the current branch's history.\n- Transplanting the last N commits: `git rebase --onto newbase HEAD~N`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7549, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7549}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo